  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on computing differences from a reference point (here, -3000) and evaluating lotteries based on the mean of these differences. The individual does not explicitly compute such differences or their probabilistic average, but they do consider the incremental loss in Lottery A (an extra 1000 Euro loss compared to B) and weigh it against the 20% chance of a better outcome (0 Euro). This reflects a qualitative consideration of outcome differences relative to the sure outcome (the reference point), which aligns with the conceptual basis of the decision reason. While there is no evidence of a formal calculation of mean differences, the individual’s deliberation centers on the same core elements—deviations from the sure outcome and their probabilistic trade-offs. The justification that 20% is not enough to risk an additional 1000 Euro implies a judgment about the unfavorable nature of the risky prospect relative to the sure one, consistent with preferring the more favorable mean difference. Therefore, although the reasoning is not formalized, the cognitive elements match closely. The individual appears to use a heuristic version of the decision reason.",
